we've seen new data which shows the  scale of ethnic minority under  representation in local government it's  the first ever complete census of  councilors and it found just seven  percent of them are from a black Asian  or minority ethnic background when in  fact they make up 14% of the population  as a whole  researchers at the University of  Manchester log the percentage of people  from an ethnic minority on each council  and this map shows exactly what they  found as you can see metropolitan or  City Council's shown here in yellow are  home to some of the UK's largest pame  populations like London and Manchester  and they're more likely to be diverse  but even many of these under represent  the communities who live there for  example only 13% of Westminster  councillors are from Abbey AME  background compared to 38% of the  population  Brighton &amp; Hove has a disparity of 11  percentage points as does quite  surprisingly Manchester there are places  where be AME people are over-represented  such as Hounslow but this is rare and  that's played a key role in  long-standing inequalities this is a  wake-up call for party leaders to say in  their localities what is the plan to  ensure that you have a membership that  looks like the people you're seeking to  serve what is the plan to ensure that  talent goes from A to B to high office  you know too often political parties  across in the fingers and hoping that  people will come forward and that they  will have a representative Parliament  it's not good enough you need to have a  comprehensive plan to recruit retain and  promote talent and once you have that  you have a representative local  authority that can serve the needs of a  diverse community the report also looks  at how many councillors from an ethnic  minority background are women it found  just 3 in 10 Asian councillors were  female and black women made up the  majority of councillors 6 out of 10 from  that community in 2019 more ethnic  minority MPs were elected to Parliament  than ever before but this progress has  masked the reality of representing  at lower levels of government finding  out why this is an issue is crucial as  councils are where power is closest to  the people and at a time of heightened  discussions around institutional racism  and inequality the lack of ethnic  representation can reinforce that  disadvantage and tackling it could  bridge the long-standing diversity gap
 

